## Core Contribution
This work decouples color restoration from brightness enhancement in low-light image processing, introducing a **retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)**-based post-processing module that corrects residual color bias by dynamically retrieving a reference image from an external knowledge base and injecting its color distribution via adaptive instance normalization. The design eliminates dependence on pixel-level correspondence through a global color vector derived from spatial-preserving histograms.

## Key Findings
- Consistent improvements in color-specific metrics across LOLv1, LOLv2-Real, and LOLv2-Synthetic benchmarks;
- Verified cross-front-end generality with four different enhancement networks (CPGA-Net++, LLFormer, FLIGHTNet, IAT);
- VGG19-based dual-index retrieval outperforms CLIP-based retrieval, confirming that textural/structural similarity—not high-level semantics—drives effective color correction.
